{"name":"Oak Ridge","hex":"#c0b0ab","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Oak Ridge","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A number of landforms in various places with this name.","A number of settlements in the United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Oak_Ridge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oak","blocks":[{"text":"An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 475 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak","wikidata":"Q12004"},{"title":"Ridge","blocks":[{"text":"A ridge is a long, narrow, elevated geomorphologic landform, structural feature, or a combination of both separated from the surrounding terrain by steep sides. The sides of a ridge slope away from a narrow top, the crest or ridgecrest, with the terrain dropping down on either side. The crest, if narrow, is also called a ridgeline. Limitations on the dimensions of a ridge are lacking. Its height above the surrounding terrain can vary from less than a meter to hundreds of meters. A ridge can be either depositional, erosional, tectonic, or a combination of these in origin and can consist of either bedrock, loose sediment, lava, or ice depending on its origin. A ridge can occur as either an isolated, independent feature or part of a larger geomorphological and/or structural feature. Frequently, a ridge can be further subdivided into smaller geomorphic or structural elements."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg/330px-%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge","wikidata":"Q740445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/500px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/500px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuercus_robur.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg/120px-Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg/250px-Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg/120px-Brockwell_Oak_%289%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrockwell_Oak_(9).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Quercus_lusitanica_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-253.jpg/120px-Quercus_lusitanica_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-253.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Quercus_lusitanica_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s